1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,190
Why does everyone use NumPy?

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:03,076
Watch what happens

3
00:00:03,076 --> 00:00:04,729
when we double a list.

4
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:06,693
Here's a plain Python list.

5
00:00:06,693 --> 00:00:07,909
To double it, Python

6
00:00:07,909 --> 00:00:09,160
visits each item,

7
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:10,662
one at a time.

8
00:00:11,012 --> 00:00:12,863
Now the NumPy way.

9
00:00:12,913 --> 00:00:13,960
a * 2 doubles

10
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:15,619
every item at once.

11
00:00:15,619 --> 00:00:16,925
The loop runs inside

12
00:00:16,925 --> 00:00:19,284
NumPy's fast, compiled code.

13
00:00:19,534 --> 00:00:20,630
Careful: nums *

14
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:22,134
2 on a plain list

15
00:00:22,134 --> 00:00:23,320
doesn't do maths.

16
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,036
It repeats the list!

17
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:26,311
Recap.

18
00:00:26,311 --> 00:00:27,584
NumPy does maths on the

19
00:00:27,584 --> 00:00:28,909
whole array at once.

20
00:00:28,909 --> 00:00:30,674
Every item shares one type.

21
00:00:30,674 --> 00:00:31,528
And a list times

22
00:00:31,528 --> 00:00:33,004
two just repeats.

23
00:00:33,354 --> 00:00:33,934
Next up:

24
00:00:33,934 --> 00:00:35,661
multidimensional arrays.

25
00:00:35,661 --> 00:00:37,598
Follow for the whole series.
